## The pre-2024 series: measured 10 August 2026

The frozen 2010--2023 index is built from `measures_metrics_newdata2023.xlsx`, which is
the EFF AI Progress Measurement data extended through 2023. An earlier draft of this
file claimed the frozen index drew only on EFF, on the strength of the `EFF/FRS18`
label in `stage2_ai_progress.py`. That label describes lineage, not contents, and the
claim was wrong: `measures.csv` itself ends in 2019, so it cannot be the source for the
last four years of the panel.

Measured provenance of the frozen source, by the URL each measurement cites:

| era | rows | citing paperswithcode.com |
|---|---|---|
| through 2017 | 1,747 | 21 (1.2%) |
| 2018--2019 | 147 | 60 (40.8%) |
| 2020--2023 | 214 | 109 (50.9%) |
| **total** | **2,108** | **190 (9.0%)** |

So Papers with Code, which is CC BY-SA 4.0, supplies about half the measurements in the
last four years of the published vintage. The dependency is real, it is inside the
paper's own index, and it is concentrated in the generative-AI period.

Two mitigations apply, and neither is a reason to be casual about it. Each row records
a factual measurement first reported in a primary paper, and most carry that paper's
own citation alongside the Papers with Code URL, so what was taken is a lookup of 190
published facts rather than a copy of a database's structure or selection. And 190 rows
is not a substantial part of the Papers with Code database in the sense the EU database
right uses. Both arguments are ours to make, however, not statements by the source, and
they are the reason the sign-off below is a real step rather than a formality.

**What we did about it, 10 August 2026.** Papers with Code was a finding aid, not a
source, and the re-citation makes that checkable rather than merely arguable. Every one
of the 190 rows is listed in `data/reference/pwc_provenance.csv` with the paper in which
its measurement was first published: 188 of the 190 name that paper, including all 109
dated 2020 or later, and 162 now carry a resolved DOI or arXiv identifier. The remaining
28 carry the paper title, which is what the attribution rests on; the link is a
convenience.

What we take is therefore a set of published facts, each attributed to its publication,
not the structure or the selection of a database. `retrieved_from` is kept in that file
as an honest record of where a value was looked up, and Papers with Code is credited in
the release for that service.

The frozen workbook is unchanged. The sidecar is generated from it by
`scripts/build_pwc_provenance_20260810.py`, which touches no value used by any index,
because editing the frozen input to win a licensing argument would be a poor trade.

Two caveats worth stating. Matching titles to DOIs is automated and was gated on the
publication year, after an ungated first pass returned a 2026 book chapter for a 2021
paper at a similarity of 1.00; the `match_confidence` column refers to that match and
never to the measurement. And the argument that facts attributed to their publications
carry no ShareAlike obligation is ours, not a statement by Papers with Code, which is
why the sign-off below is still worth having.

**EFF.** The project's own `LICENSE.md` dual-licenses under CC BY-SA 4.0 and GPLv2+,
and then draws the line we need, verbatim:

> "Much of this Notebook is uncopyrightable data."

The licence covers EFF's written content and source code; the benchmark measurements
are declared uncopyrightable by the source itself, an organisation whose subject is
copyright law. We take the measurements, not the prose and not the notebook code, so
the ShareAlike condition attaches to material we neither use nor redistribute. We
attribute EFF and Felten, Raj and Seamans regardless, as scholarly practice rather than
as a licence condition.

**Papers with Code.** CC BY-SA 4.0, with no equivalent uncopyrightable-data statement.
It is the recorded retrieval point for 190 rows of the frozen source, concentrated in
2018--2023, and it also feeds the 2024 refresh path. Each of those rows is now
attributed to the paper that published the measurement (see above), so the release
carries published facts with their citations rather than the Papers with Code database.

**Direction of travel.** Epoch AI (CC BY 4.0) supplies everything admitted at the 2024
chain point, and the admission rule in DOCUMENTATION.md section 3 requires a
redistribution-clean licence of every series added since. The BY-SA dependency does not
grow; the re-citation cleanup can shrink it.

**Required before the DOI is minted.** Confirmation from ORU research support that the
position above is sound: CC BY 4.0 on the derived scores, on the basis that the 190
Papers with Code rows are published measurements attributed to their publications rather
than an extract of that database. The re-citation makes this a much easier question than
it was this morning, but it is still ours to have checked rather than to assert, and a
Zenodo DOI cannot be quietly withdrawn.

